@jcomp11

Yeah, but a large segment of our society has seemingly accepted an update to the public lexicon, in which the term “AI” often means something like, “generative AI-powered technology”.

This is really quite a coup for the genAI industry, as decades’ worth of associations (particularly from sci-fi novels and movies) with “AI” are coming along for the ride, despite the fact that genAI really does *not* much resemble the kind of “AI” humans have dreamt about building for the last ~80 years (although for much of the non-technical public, the magnitude of that delta is obscured by the linguistic interface of LLMs).

@jcomp11

Actually I can substitute a term because I feel like it, but it would be silly to do so if only I understood what I meant.  But if I'm able to communicate successfully my meaning, there's no real problem.
However, my aim is to show people that "artificial intelligence" is an oxymoron.  People may, and probably will, continue to use the term, but my goal is to get people to think about it more than they otherwise would.

